1
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:58,500
How much is enough, Barquero?

2
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Barquero is a western film directed by Gordon Douglas.

3
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:43,500
Jake Remy leads a gang of outlaw cutthroats making their
escape toward Mexico from a successful robbery.

4
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:36,000
Barring their way is a river--crossable only by means of a ferry barge.

5
00:34:22,500 --> 00:34:28,500
The barge operator, Travis, refuses to be bullied into providing transport for the gang and escapes across river
with most of the local populace--leaving Remy and his gang behind, desperately seeking a way across.

6
00:41:15,000 --> 00:41:21,000
A river-wide stand-off begins between the gang and the townspeople, both
groups of which have left people on the wrong side of the river.

7
00:48:07,500 --> 00:48:13,500
Travis, played by Lee Van Cleef.

8
00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:06,000
Jake Remy, played by Warren Oates.

9
01:01:52,500 --> 01:01:58,500
Mountain Phil, played by Forrest Tucker.

10
01:08:45,000 --> 01:08:51,000
Marquette, played by Kerwin Mathews.

11
01:15:37,500 --> 01:15:43,500
Anna, played by Mariette Hartley.

12
01:22:30,000 --> 01:22:36,000
Nola, played by Marie Gomez.

13
01:29:22,500 --> 01:29:28,500
Barquero is a 1970 American Western film starring Lee Van Cleef and Warren
Oates, produced by Hal Klein and directed by Gordon Douglas.

14
01:36:15,000 --> 01:36:21,000
Barquero was Lee Van Cleef's first American-made film
since 1962's How the West Was Won.

15
01:43:07,500 --> 01:43:13,500
It was also his first starring role in an American Western.
